    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nAll rejection responses (400, 409, 429) now return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`, while HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\n**What was wrong:** Nine escalation handlers under `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/` were still emitting plain `ResponseEntity<String>` (or, for missing fields, a raw `{\"errors\":[...]}` JSON map) instead of problem+json. Only the bean-validation handler already produced RFC7807.\n\n**What I changed** \u2014 converted each of these handlers to build a `ProblemDetail` via the existing `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper and send it as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` (the same pattern already used by `ValidationExceptionHandler`, `ForbiddenExceptionHandler`, `GeneralExceptionHandler`):\n\n- **400:** `DisposableEmailDomainHandler`, `FutureRegistrationDateHandler`, `InvalidEmailHandler`, `InvalidPostcodeHandler`, `InvalidTelephoneHandler`, `MissingOwnerFieldsHandler` (kept its field list as an extra `errors` member alongside the standard problem fields)\n- **409:** `CityAtCapacityHandler`, `DuplicateIdentityHandler`\n- **429:** `DailyLimitExceededHandler`\n\n`ProblemDetails.build` supplies `type` (`about:blank`), `title` (exception simple name), `status` (from `ProblemDetail.forStatus`), and `detail` (the exception message). The OfficeFloor starter routes the response through Spring's return-value handlers, so a `ProblemDetail` body gets the `application/problem+json` content type automatically.\n\n**Verification:** The existing `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` passes. I also ran a temporary test (since removed) that triggered a 400 (invalid postcode + missing fields), a 409 (city at capacity), and a 429 (daily limit) and confirmed each returns `application/problem+json` with all four required members present \u2014 then re-ran the full suite green.",
